"""Version-pinned editorial replacements for draft-07 v0.7.3.

These are authored changes, not a claim that every historical assertion in the
series has been independently verified. apply_editorial_20261116.py validates
all anchors and input blobs before writing any chapter.
"""
PATCHES = []

def p(part, issue, anchor, text):
    PATCHES.append(dict(part=part, issue=issue, mode='paragraph', anchor=anchor, text=text))

def r(part, issue, anchor, text):
    PATCHES.append(dict(part=part, issue=issue, mode='replace', anchor=anchor, text=text))

def s(part, issue, anchor, end, text):
    PATCHES.append(dict(part=part, issue=issue, mode='span', anchor=anchor, end=end, text=text))

def a(part, issue, anchor, text):
    PATCHES.append(dict(part=part, issue=issue, mode='after', anchor=anchor, text=text))

# Prologue: the evidence, the ruined physical place, and the limits of the frame.
p(0, 'P0-ORIENTATION', 'The year was 401 BCE.', "The year was 401 BCE. Put that in the world of classical Greece, the Greece of Athens and Sparta. Socrates is still alive. The famous stand of the Spartans at Thermopylae happened about eighty years earlier. We are already thousands of years after the first cities, but still more than four centuries before the beginning of the calendar we use today.")
s(0, 'P0-RUINS', 'This was farming country,', '[VISUAL: Map — Nimrud', """This was river country, with fields and villages. Among them the army passed the remains of a much larger place, a city Xenophon calls deserted.

He describes a wall of clay brick on a stone base. He puts it at a hundred feet high and twenty-five feet thick. A hundred feet is roughly a ten-story building. Those are his reported dimensions, not a modern survey, but the impression is unmistakable: an enormous amount of building still stood above him. Nearby was a tall stone structure he calls a pyramid. People from the surrounding villages had climbed onto it to get away from the passing army.

What would the ruined city actually have looked like? We have to imagine some of that. Don't picture clean archaeological foundations with paths between them. Picture fallen brick gathering against the foot of a wall, its upper edges worn into irregular shapes. Where roofs had burned or fallen, rain could get into rooms that had once been protected. Mud brick can stand for centuries when it is maintained; left exposed, it gradually washes and crumbles back into earth. Stone footings can keep their outline after much of the building above them has gone.

And the wood? Some may have burned in the city's destruction. Useful beams and doors could have been carried away. Other timber could decay, while buried or charred fragments survived. A dry summer doesn't preserve an entire city like a sealed tomb. This landscape has wet seasons too. We can't reconstruct the condition of each house on the day Xenophon passed, but a standing wall did not mean that a habitable city remained behind it.

A day's march later the Greeks reached another deserted stronghold, larger than the first. Here Xenophon noticed shells in the dressed stone at the base of the wall.

Did he go inside? His account gives us no tour of the streets or houses. Nor does it name the people from whom he obtained the stories he records. We shouldn't turn that brief passage into an interview with the last inhabitants. We do know there were people living in the surrounding country; the villagers seeking shelter are right there in his account.

The stories are strange. The first city, he says, had belonged to the Medes, a people from the Iranian mountains. A Persian king had failed to take it until a cloud hid the sun and its defenders lost heart. The second city had a similar story, this time with thunder from Zeus. Xenophon recorded them and continued the march. He had an army to get home.

""")
p(0, 'P0-NINEVEH', 'You may not know those names,', "Nineveh had been a capital of Assyria, a kingdom centered on the Tigris River in what is now northern Iraq. Its kings conquered far beyond that homeland, reaching into Egypt. Nineveh became one of the great cities of the ancient world, a place of palaces, temples, and royal administration. The ruins belonged to a power that had once commanded much of the country through which these Greeks were struggling.")
p(0, 'P0-SACK', 'In 612 BCE its enemies', "In 612 BCE its enemies sacked it. Its life as the capital of that empire was over.")
p(0, 'P0-MEMORY', 'But look at the story Xenophon was given.', "But look at the stories Xenophon records. They have Medes in them, and Persians, and a thunderstorm. They don't identify the Assyrian kings who had made these cities great. An educated Greek was passing the remains of an immense empire, and his account preserved a very incomplete explanation of what he saw.")
p(0, 'P0-MEMORY-LIMIT', 'I should be careful.', "That tells us something about the account, not everything about what people along the river remembered. Some knowledge and settlement survived the empire. What unsettles me is the gap: the masonry remained visible long after an accurate history of it had become difficult for this traveler to obtain.")
r(0, 'P0-ANCIENT-CITY', 'all of that was ancient before Nineveh was built.', 'all of that was ancient before Nineveh became an imperial capital.')
p(0, 'P0-SERIES-FRAME', 'We call that older world the Bronze Age.', "We call that older world the Bronze Age. This series follows its development in the Near East and the eastern Mediterranean, from the growth of cities to an age of kings exchanging letters and ships carrying goods from distant countries. There were loans, contracts, customer complaints, and a peace treaty with an extradition clause. Around 1200 BCE, many of its kingdoms suffered profound disruption. Some cities burned, some governments disappeared, and some communities carried on. What survived, and what became difficult to remember, depended on where you stood.")
p(0, 'P0-QUESTION', "So here's the question I'd like you to carry with you.", "So here's the question I'd like you to carry with you. How do people build a world that connected, live in it for centuries, and then lose so much of its working machinery? And why can one community keep its records while another eventually tells stories about the people who built the ruins?")
p(0, 'P0-ROLES', 'Watch what happens to those five kinds of work.', "Watch what happens to those five kinds of work. Some remain within a household. Others come to depend on people across a sea or beyond a range of mountains. Those connections make new kinds of wealth possible. They also create problems when the goods, payments, or protection people expect stop arriving. Following the work will help us see both the strength of that world and the different ways parts of it broke.")
p(0, 'P0-5000-SCOPE', 'There are no cities yet, anywhere.', "We are before the great cities and written archives of this story. There's a small house that smells of smoke and sheep, and in it someone is about to wake up.")

# Part 1: keep the repaired household and the narrator's argument; remove false universals.
p(1, 'P1-HOUSEHOLD-DAY', "And there's your sister, the boy's mother.", "And there's your sister, the boy's mother. She works the field beside you at harvest and does much of the work that keeps the household fed. Right now she's on her knees at the grinding stone, pushing one rock across another to turn grain into meal. Fine dust gathers around the stone. This is one long task in a day that also includes cooking and other work, not the only thing she ever does. She's the best baker in the village and she knows it. You've stopped hearing the scrape.")
p(1, 'P1-CITY-SCOPE', "There's no city, anywhere on Earth.", "There is no city in the world this household knows. The biggest crowd you've stood in might be a seasonal gathering of a few hundred people. Your familiar country is the land you can walk across and return from. Your brother has been further. Beyond that, you depend on other people's stories.")
p(1, 'P1-STATE-SCOPE', "There's no king and nobody to pay taxes to.", "In this imagined village there is no palace collecting a regular share of the harvest. There are people others listen to. Your grandmother is one. A stronger village could raid you. Authority and inequality haven't waited for cities to appear, but the distant officials and written demands we will meet later are not part of this household's day.")
r(1, 'P1-AMERICAN-FOODS', 'Those are on the other side of an ocean nobody will cross for six and a half thousand years.', 'Those crops come from the Americas and will not enter this region\'s kitchens until thousands of years later.')
p(1, 'P1-FARMING-START', 'Twelve thousand years ago, nobody farmed.', "About twelve thousand years ago, near the end of the last Ice Age, people in parts of southwest Asia were beginning the long transition toward farming. Hunting and gathering had sustained human communities for vastly longer. So why begin changing a way of getting food that already worked?")
p(1, 'P1-HUNGER', 'The usual guess is hunger.', "Hunger is one possible answer. More people, fewer animals to hunt, a changing climate: any of those could make a new food supply attractive. But farming began in different places under different conditions, and a simple story of starving people being forced to invent it leaves too much out.")
p(1, 'P1-HARLAN-INFERENCE', 'So in those hills, at least,', "Harlan's experiment doesn't tell us what every ancient household could harvest. It shows why we shouldn't picture all gatherers as people barely scratching a living from an empty landscape. Some wild resources were extraordinarily productive. That makes planting a crop something to explain, not an obvious improvement.")
p(1, 'P1-WEALTH', 'People had owned things before this:', "People had stored food and valued possessions before farming. Grain adds a particularly useful kind of reserve: a pile that can be counted, divided, lent, and taken. In some places, stores like these will become part of much larger institutions. It takes a very long time, and a bin does not turn into a kingdom by itself.")
p(1, 'P1-SKELETONS', 'In a lot of places, farming made people', "In many places, the transition to farming came with serious costs. Skeletal studies often find poorer childhood nutrition, dental disease, and heavy repetitive work. The pattern varies between communities, and a worn joint doesn't identify a person's exact daily chore. But think again about the work before breakfast in our house: grain has to become food, one batch at a time. A successful harvest can still demand an exhausting life.")
p(1, 'P1-CATAL-SOUND', "I can't tell you what those people believed.", "We can't recover exactly what those people believed. But picture the practical business of that town: someone crossing a roof with a load, a ladder leading down into a room, a hearth needing fuel, grain needing grinding. The dead beneath the floor belonged to a space in which the living were still cooking, working, and sleeping.")
p(1, 'P1-RETURN', 'Which brings us back to your morning.', "Which brings us back to our imagined household around 5000 BCE. Five people are at home; the brother who trades is away. Now the bins and the grinding stone have a history behind them. They are part of how this family makes it through the year.")

# Part 2: navigation and logistics, with an explicit illustrative route.
p(2, 'P2-MATERIALS', 'It hardly ever rains there.', "Rainfall on the southern plain is too low for dependable farming without river water. There is little local building stone, no convenient supply of long construction timbers, and no local metal ore. But the mud and reeds are useful materials, not merely things the villagers are stuck with. You can make walls, roofs, boats, containers, and eventually writing surfaces from what this landscape provides.")
r(2, 'P2-CAUSALITY', "There's a catch, and the catch made everything else happen.", "There's a catch that helps explain the work a settlement needs to organize.")
p(2, 'P2-CANAL-AUTHORITY', 'And who in the village already settles things?', "Somebody has to settle those arguments. That doesn't tell us by itself whether the answer is a council, a powerful household, a temple, or a king. Irrigation creates a practical problem of cooperation; it isn't a machine that automatically produces one kind of government.")
p(2, 'P2-FREIGHT', 'Water for the fields is part of it.', "Water for the fields is part of it. Transport is another part. A loaded donkey can carry roughly a hundred and fifty pounds, depending on the animal and the journey. The animals and their handlers also need food and water, and a long trip costs time and labor. Boats can move much heavier loads with fewer people. Downstream the current helps; upstream a boat may need towing, paddling, or a favorable wind. River transport isn't effortless, but it changes what is practical to bring into a city.")
p(2, 'P2-CITY-FOOD', 'A city is tens of thousands of people', "A large city needs food arriving day after day, much of it grown beyond the houses its inhabitants can see. Waterways made that easier in southern Iraq. They are an important advantage here, not a rule that explains the location of every early city.")
p(2, 'P2-BARLEY-DEVICE', "Let's follow some.", "Let's follow an imagined load of barley. The route and the people are a way into the work; they are not a surviving account of one particular delivery.")
p(2, 'P2-ARRIVAL', 'For the Carrier, who grew up', "Picture the Carrier arriving from a small village. He passes animals, cooking fires, workshops, loads being carried toward the water. Smoke and river mud are familiar smells; the number of people is not. The layout is harder to take in than a village where every path leads to someone he knows. A city is a new problem for his eyes before it becomes an idea in his head.")
r(2, 'P2-URUK-SCALE', 'either figure makes it the biggest gathering of human beings there has ever been.', 'either figure makes it an extraordinary concentration of people for its time.')
p(2, 'P2-GRAIN-SCOPE', "So here's my answer. It's one answer,", "So here's one part of my answer. Copper alone doesn't make a city. Large populations need food supplies and ways of organizing work beyond a single household. Stored grain made that possible in particular ways at Uruk. It does not follow that every society without an Uruk lacked the resources, intelligence, or institutions to build one.")
r(2, 'P2-CORN-SCOPE', "The one crop that could do grain's job in North America was corn,", 'One crop that became particularly important to large settlements in North America was corn,')

# Part 3: evidence categories, the empire's ordinary business, and climatic scope.
p(3, 'P3-FOUNDLING-DATE', 'The copies we have of that story', "The surviving copies of Sargon's birth legend are from the first millennium BCE, long after the historical king. The setting of a story and the date someone wrote it down are different things. We can compare its floating basket with the Moses tradition without pretending we have a contemporary record of Sargon's infancy.")
p(3, 'P3-CONQUEST-NOVELTY', "Sargon didn't go home.", "Sargon's conquests became a sustained project of ruling distant cities. His inscriptions describe appointed governors, military victories, and resources gathered toward Akkad. Earlier rulers had already imposed authority beyond their own city walls. The change here is the scale and duration of the effort, not the invention of conquest in a single man's lifetime.")
p(3, 'P3-ORDINARY-AGENCY', "Nobody asked him. There isn't even a word yet", "Picture what that change asks of a household. The grain must still be threshed, measured, and carried out. Someone has to argue about the amount, find labor for the load, and decide what remains for the family. The new ruler can be remote while the demand is close enough to put a hand into the grain jar.")
p(3, 'P3-ADMINISTRATION', "Sargon's people had no manual.", "Sargon's officials could draw on generations of city government. Now they had to adapt that experience to a much larger territory, with more languages, longer journeys, and more places that might refuse an order.")
p(3, 'P3-ENHEDUANNA-TEXTS', 'For seven hundred years writing had been used', "Under Enheduanna's name we have fierce, personal hymns to the goddess Inanna. They survive in copies made centuries after her lifetime, and scholars disagree about how much of the poetry goes back to her. Her historical position is secure; the relationship between that woman and every line attributed to her is harder to establish. The works made her name part of a literary tradition, not just a royal family tree.")
p(3, 'P3-INTERPRETER-CHRONOLOGY', 'Sargon bragged that ships from three distant lands', "Sargon boasted that ships from distant lands reached his capital. One trading partner was Meluhha, generally identified with the Indus civilization, far to the east. There is a later seal, from around the end of the third millennium BCE, belonging to a man called Shu-ilishu. Its inscription calls him an interpreter of Meluhha. It doesn't put him at Sargon's own dock. It gives us something more modest and wonderfully human: someone whose job was to make that distant trade understandable.")
p(3, 'P3-INTERPRETER', 'So somewhere in this empire there was a man', "Someone spoke both sides of a conversation that we can now recover from only one side. To him it was work.")
p(3, 'P3-EXILE-SOURCE', 'And during that great revolt,', "The poetry attributed to Enheduanna also tells a story of political violence at Ur. Its speaker says a rebel has forced her from her religious office. We cannot turn all its details into a securely dated eyewitness account of Naram-Sin's revolt, but we can hear how it presents the experience of losing power.")
p(3, 'P3-EXILE-PERSON', 'Enheduanna was an old woman by then.', "The voice in the poem is no longer simply a royal daughter occupying an important office. She is someone pleading for that position and her good name to be restored. The institution that made her powerful has also made her vulnerable.")
p(3, 'P3-EXILE-OUTCOME', 'It worked, or something did.', "The poem presents a restoration of her position. That is the story the work tells; the political sequence behind it is less certain.")
p(3, 'P3-ENHEDUANNA-VERDICT', "She's one of the most remarkable people", "What holds my attention is the combination: a real royal daughter in a powerful religious office, and a later literary tradition that speaks in her name with extraordinary force. We don't need to know every stage of that tradition to hear a person rather than another total of barley.")
p(3, 'P3-CLIMATE', "There's a physical clue. Drill a core", "There are physical clues to environmental stress around this period: dust in marine sediments, changes in settlement, and records preserved in caves and lakes. Some point to severe drying in parts of the region around 2200 BCE. They do not give us a single weather report for every province, and the timing of the Akkadian state's political decline does not fit every climatic record neatly.")
p(3, 'P3-CLIMATE-INFERENCE', "It's tempting to say that's the answer.", "Drought may have made existing conflicts and provisioning problems harder to manage. That is a plausible connection, not a complete explanation recovered from a ring of dust. Different districts could face different pressures, and the documents and the ground do not let us isolate one cause of the empire's end.")
p(3, 'P3-EGYPT-COMPARISON', 'Now the side trip, because the same thing', "Now a side trip to Egypt, where a different state faced its own period of fragmentation. The comparison is useful, but we should not assume that similar-looking endings prove a single simultaneous cause.")
p(3, 'P3-NAMED-LIVES', "The women doing the Farmer's sister's kind of work", "The women doing the Farmer's sister's kind of work appear in these administrative records through obligations, rations, and labor. Sometimes ancient records do preserve individual names. What we usually lack is an account in the woman's own voice of the life behind the entry.")
p(3, 'P3-UR-LETTERS', 'We have the letters, in copies made later.', "The exchange survives in later copies, part of the literary memory of Ur's fall. It presents a king unable to make an official deliver urgently needed grain. Whether every accusation is reliable or not, the practical problem is clear: commanding a shipment and getting it to the city are different things.")

# Part 4: documentary relationships and an intelligible lending example.
p(4, 'P4-LETTER-JOURNEY', 'Lamassi lives in a city called Assur,', "Lamassi lives at Assur, on the Tigris in northern Iraq. Pushu-ken is at Kanesh, on the plateau of central Turkey. Between the two homes lie hundreds of miles of road, river crossings, and mountains. A journey can take weeks. The letter travels with people already moving goods; a reply needs a messenger going the other way. Imagine waiting for an answer while the wool still has to be bought and the household still has to eat. Later Assyrian kings will rule a vast empire from this northern homeland. For now our story is about a merchant city and families doing business abroad.")
p(4, 'P4-SISTERS', "The wives' letters are the best things", "The family letters are sharp. One wife complains about being left with an empty house while her husband makes a life abroad. In another exchange, it is two sisters addressing their brother: he loves money, they say, and hates his own life. The relationship matters. This is a wider family trying to get an absent man to attend to obligations at home, not another quarrel between Lamassi and her husband.")
p(4, 'P4-JAIL-SEQUENCE', 'I love that. And read Lamassi', "The letters don't let us line up every complaint and arrest as one continuous domestic scene. But they do let us see how much business was being managed at the other end of the route, while the man abroad was sending instructions home.")
p(4, 'P4-ALPHABET-ORIGIN', "There are short inscriptions scratched on the rocks", "Short inscriptions around the Sinai mines use just such a principle. Related early alphabetic inscriptions also come from Egypt itself. Their dates and the identities of their writers are debated, so we should not award the invention to a named crew we cannot identify. What matters here is the experiment: a small set of signs representing sounds, adapted from a much more elaborate writing tradition.")
p(4, 'P4-DEBT-EXAMPLE', 'He borrows grain in the spring,', "He may borrow grain because the old harvest is running out before the new one is ready. Old Babylonian documents commonly use annual rates of twenty percent for silver and about a third for barley, though actual terms vary. For a simple one-year illustration, three baskets borrowed at a third become four baskets owed. That is not a claim that every spring loan charged a full year's interest by June. The danger for this borrower is that his repayment depends on a harvest he cannot guarantee.")
p(4, 'P4-DEBT-CAUSALITY', 'Run that for a generation and you get', "Debt can transfer land, labor, and bargaining power toward creditors. Kings have reasons to intervene beyond sympathy: a household stripped of its resources may no longer be able to supply taxes, soldiers, or other obligations. The private creditor's gain can become the ruler's problem.")
r(4, 'P4-DEBT-VISUAL', '[VISUAL: Diagram — a farmer\'s year: borrow grain in spring (price high), repay at harvest (price low), "plus one third."]', '[VISUAL: Diagram — an explicitly illustrative one-year barley loan: three baskets borrowed, four owed at a one-third annual rate. Actual contract terms vary.]')
r(4, 'P4-TIME-RETURN', "We've come forward about a century from the merchants, and back down to the river plain.", "Leave Nanni's complaint for a moment and go back a few decades, to the beginning of Hammurabi's reign. We are on the river plain again, about a century after the merchant households we began with.")

# Part 5: the working ship, the right political categories, and source attribution.
p(5, 'P5-WRECK', "We don't know what happened. That cape", "We don't know exactly how the ship was lost. Her cargo came to rest down a steep underwater slope, where it remained for more than three thousand years. The wreck gives us objects and their positions; it cannot replay the last minutes of the voyage.")
a(5, 'P5-HOLD', "It's in three hundred and fifty-four slabs,", "Picture the practical problem of working around that cargo. Each slab is roughly the weight of a heavy suitcase. Jars have to be secured against movement; loads need to be placed so the vessel can sail safely. This is a reconstruction of the work, but the weight is real. Those beautiful objects in museum cases were once freight that somebody had to lift.")
p(5, 'P5-ROLES-ABOARD', "So five people who started out in one room", "The wreck brings our five kinds of work together. It does not identify five particular passengers. It shows how a cargo can depend on farmers, craftspeople, record-keepers, merchants, and powerful customers who may never meet.")
p(5, 'P5-TIN-PROVENANCE', 'Now the tin. I promised you', "Now the tin. Researchers have compared the metal in these ingots with ores from different regions, using chemical composition and isotopes. A study published in 2022 proposed sources in Central Asia and the Taurus Mountains of Turkey; other researchers have challenged those assignments. The broad point is safer than a map with a precise route drawn on it. This was an international trade in material whose origins are difficult to reconstruct, even with the cargo in our hands.")
p(5, 'P5-SHIPPER-KNOWLEDGE', 'Whoever loaded that tin knew exactly', "We don't have the shipment's full paperwork. Its merchants may have bought through intermediaries rather than dealing with the miners themselves. The ingots preserve the metal, not every conversation that moved it toward the coast.")
p(5, 'P5-ASSYRIA', "Off to the side there's Assyria,", "In northern Iraq is Assyria, a kingdom with its old center at Assur, the city of the donkey merchants. During these generations its rulers are emerging from Mitanni's dominance and asserting themselves among the great powers. Remember the distinction: Assur is the city; Assyria is the kingdom whose power is growing around it.")
p(5, 'P5-AKKADIAN', 'They wrote in Babylonian.', "Much of the correspondence was written in Babylonian, a form of Akkadian spoken in Mesopotamia. For courts such as Egypt's and the Hittites', it served as an international language learned by scribes. Think of aviation English: the shared language makes the exchange possible even when the people on either side speak different languages at home.")
r(5, 'P5-ASSYRIA-RETURN', 'from Assyria, the merchant city nobody was watching.', 'from the rising Assyrian kingdom in northern Iraq.')
p(5, 'P5-KADESH-ATTRIBUTION', 'Fifty years after the plague began,', "Several decades later, Egypt and the Hittites fought at Kadesh, a fortified city beside the Orontes River in what is now Syria. Most of the detailed battle story comes from Ramses's own inscriptions and pictures. They were made to celebrate him. We can follow that account without mistaking it for a neutral report from both armies.")
r(5, 'P5-ASSYRIA-TREATY', 'Assyria, the merchant city, had become an army.', 'the Assyrian kingdom had become a formidable military rival.')
p(5, 'P5-PYLOS-ANNUAL', "His tablets aren't meant to last.", "The tablets are working records, usually written on clay and left unbaked. Many concern a short period of administration rather than a permanent history. Imagine a clerk setting down a tablet after checking a delivery. It can dry hard enough to handle without becoming fired pottery. That difference will matter when we return to this archive.")
p(5, 'P5-PYLOS-LITERACY', 'And his skill has one customer.', "The surviving Linear B records are overwhelmingly administrative. We have no surviving body of letters or poems in this script. That doesn't let us inspect every person's abilities in the kingdom, but it does show how closely the evidence for this kind of writing is tied to palace work. Lose that work and the training of new scribes becomes much harder to sustain.")
p(5, 'P5-WOMENS-STATUS', 'Some of the women on his lists', "Some groups of women are identified by place names across the Aegean, in what is now Turkey. Scholars have argued that some were captives or otherwise dependent workers. The labels do not give us one clear legal status for every woman. They show people being administered through work and rations, with very little of their own story attached.")
p(5, 'P5-UGARIT-ALPHABET', 'He hears four languages between the ship', "Imagine several languages being spoken around him. Ugarit's scribes used more than one language and more than one writing system. Among them was an alphabet of about thirty signs, written in wedges on clay. It was used for serious scribal work, including letters, religious texts, and accounts. Keep that in mind: alphabetic writing was already useful before the palaces of this world fell.")
p(5, 'P5-SINGER', "He stops at a doorway. Inside there's a woman", "Put a singer in a doorway as the working day ends. We cannot recover this particular woman or her song; she belongs to our imagined evening. But a hymn to the goddess Nikkal really does survive among Ugarit's tablets, with signs interpreted as musical instructions. The archives contain more than prices and royal demands.")
p(5, 'P5-UNEQUAL-PROSPERITY', 'Each of them is better off than his ancestors', "This world offers possibilities the first household never had: specialized crafts, distant markets, ambitious literature, goods from beyond the horizon. The gains are not shared equally. A powerful ruler, an independent merchant, and a dependent weaver occupy very different positions. What connects them is the work that has to keep being done: harvests, shipments, agreements, and payments.")
p(5, 'P5-KNOWING-DANGER', "I don't think anyone inside it could see that.", "The people inside it knew about bad harvests, hostile neighbors, and failed deliveries. Their letters say so. What they could not know was which troubles would be overcome, as many had been before, and which institutions would not survive the next generations.")

# Part 6: the map must explain how relief actually reaches a household.
p(6, 'P6-GRAIN-DATE', 'Sometime around the year 1220 BCE', "Late in the thirteenth century BCE, decades after Egypt and the Hittites had made peace, an urgent letter went from the Hittite court to Ugarit, the trading kingdom on the Syrian coast.")
p(6, 'P6-GRAIN-ORDER', "It's an order. Ugarit is", "It's an order for a ship and a crew to move a large quantity of grain from a Syrian region to a Hittite harbor. The ancient measures are not a reason to pretend we know the cargo's weight to the ton. What matters is the urgency. In effect, the letter says that the shipment is a matter of life or death.")
r(6, 'P6-CONFIDENCE', "what was going wrong, and why nobody was worried.", 'what was going wrong, and what these kingdoms already knew how to survive.')
r(6, 'P6-ASSYRIA', 'Assyria, the merchant city.', 'Assyria, the northern Iraqi kingdom centered on Assur.')
p(6, 'P6-MITANNI-SCOPE', 'And nothing happened.', "The rest of the international order did not collapse with it. There had been war, displacement, and a struggle over who would control the former kingdom. But routes could be redirected and new rulers could take over relationships. Assyria gained a place among the great powers. Mitanni's disappearance showed that a major political break did not have to end every connection around it.")
p(6, 'P6-WEATHER-TIME', 'Now forward, and what was going wrong.', "Now follow the environmental evidence forward, all the way to the years around 1200 BCE. After that we will return to the decisions kings were making in the preceding decades. The evidence doesn't arrive in the order an ancient household would have experienced it.")
r(6, 'P6-NILE', "He doesn't have a river like the Nile that floods no matter what.", "He cannot rely on a great river to compensate for poor local rain. Even the Nile's floods could fail, but his own fields depend much more directly on rainfall here.")
r(6, 'P6-INVENTED-NUMBER', 'It was happening in a hundred thousand houses.', 'The evidence does not give us a count of households facing that exact choice. The imagined jar makes the stakes understandable; it is not another measurement in the study.')
r(6, 'P6-WALL-MATERIAL', 'Four miles of stone wall ran around it,', 'Its defenses stretched for miles, with substantial stone foundations and mud-brick construction above,')
p(6, 'P6-SILO-TIME', 'At that capital archaeologists found', "Excavations at Hattusa have revealed very large grain-storage installations, including some built centuries before these final crises. They show how seriously the state had invested in reserves. They do not tell us exactly how full the capital's stores were in the year of our letter. A bin's capacity and the food actually left inside it are different facts.")
p(6, 'P6-GRAIN-ROUTE', 'When the bins ran low, they bought.', "The letters and royal inscriptions show efforts to obtain grain from outside. Keep the shipments separate: the urgent Ugarit order concerns a cargo from a Syrian region, while Egyptian records describe other grain sent to Hatti. And reaching a harbor was only one stage. The Hittite capital stood far inland on a high plateau. After unloading, someone still had to organize animals, carts, labor, and food for the journey uphill. A ship on the coast was not yet dinner in Hattusa.")
p(6, 'P6-EMBARGO-TIME', 'By the 1230s the Hittites', "Go back now to the later thirteenth century, before those three exceptionally dry years. Assyria was a major rival to the east, with territory taken from the old horse kingdom. The Hittite court was trying to control what could reach it through dependent lands.")
p(6, 'P6-MIGRATION-CAUSE', "I think the simplest way to see it is this.", "One possibility is that failed harvests helped push some people toward new homes or new ways of making a living. War and political displacement could do that too. A family on the move, a hired soldier, and a raiding crew need not belong to three permanently separate worlds. But the evidence does not identify the motive of every group, or establish the same drought on every island and coast.")
r(6, 'P6-ADVISER-SILOS', "They'd built the biggest silos in their history.", "Grain storage was already part of the state's practice.")
r(6, 'P6-ADVISER-LAST-KING', 'Their last king is about to go and fight for them himself.', 'A later Hittite king would report fighting at sea himself.')
p(6, 'P6-COUNTER-CONCEPTS', "He doesn't have a word for what he's looking at.", "He can recognize the shortfall. His difficulty is deciding what will fix it, and persuading people elsewhere to provide what the stores need. Knowing that trouble is serious is not the same as having a remedy that still works.")

# Part 7: retain the drama without converting partial records into total collapse.
p(7, 'P7-AMMURAPI-AGE', 'The last king of Ugarit was a young man', "The last known king of Ugarit was named Ammurapi. Among the letters from these troubled years is one full of good advice.")
p(7, 'P7-SETTING', 'You know Ugarit.', "You know Ugarit from the imagined evening at its harbor. The capital stood a short distance inland from the Syrian coast; its port lay in a nearby bay. Cargo had to move between ship, shore, and city. The royal palace and archives belonged to a prosperous kingdom, but its king answered to the Hittite rulers in what is now Turkey.")
r(7, 'P7-FATHER-TITLE', 'an older ally he addressed as "my father."', 'a ruler he addressed diplomatically as "my father." That title does not tell us the man\'s age.')
p(7, 'P7-LETTER-ORDER', 'The answer he got is the letter full of advice.', "One surviving letter from the king of Cyprus offers advice. I'll paraphrase it. If enemy ships have been seen, make yourself strong. Gather your troops, secure your towns, and watch for the enemy. The letters do not come with a complete, securely dated sequence of replies, so we should not pretend we can follow the messengers day by day.")
p(7, 'P7-REPLY', "It's sound. It's also not much use,", "A letter from Ammurapi shows why such advice might be desperately difficult to follow. He reports enemy ships damaging his territory while his own forces are elsewhere.")
p(7, 'P7-SEVEN-SHIPS', 'Seven ships is perhaps three hundred men.', "Seven ships. The king gives us that number, but no reliable count of the men aboard. He reports attacks on his territory; he does not establish that these were the same crews that finally destroyed the capital. What we can hear plainly is his predicament: the forces he needed at home were serving elsewhere.")
p(7, 'P7-IMAGINED-RAIDER', 'Put yourself on one of those seven ships.', "Picture one possible man aboard a raiding ship: someone who once worked a field and can no longer feed his household from it. A boat offers work, escape, or the chance to take food from someone else. That man is imagined. He lets us consider a possible route into raiding, not calculate the odds that a particular attacker had been a farmer.")
p(7, 'P7-IMAGINATION-LIMIT', "I can't prove that's who they were.", "The families shown in Egyptian battle imagery will give us another reason to think about people on the move. They do not identify the men in Ammurapi's letter. Keep the possibility, and keep the two pieces of evidence separate.")
p(7, 'P7-ALLIES', "That's the answer to the second question", "Why didn't help arrive in time? Ugarit's obligations give us one part of an answer: a subordinate kingdom could be required to send troops and ships away. That made this coast vulnerable. It does not prove that every ally was helpless for the same reason, or that every surviving plea went unanswered.")
p(7, 'P7-DELIVERY', 'I prefer the real version.', "The surviving tablet is not proof of a message trapped in an oven. A retained copy is one possible explanation for a letter remaining at Ugarit. But finding it there does not tell us whether a messenger arrived, what answer came back, or what help anyone attempted.")
p(7, 'P7-DESTRUCTION', 'Ugarit burned. The layer of ash', "Ugarit suffered a violent destruction. Excavators found burned and collapsed buildings, weapons, and buried valuables. Picture what a collapsing upper floor can do to the rooms below: crush furniture, scatter stored objects, and cover an archive in rubble. That is a way to understand the remains, not a recovered account of fighting from one doorway to the next.")
p(7, 'P7-NO-RETURN', 'Nobody came back.', "The great Late Bronze Age capital was not rebuilt as it had been. Its palaces and archives ceased to function. We cannot turn that into a statement about the fate of every person who had lived there. Centuries of later activity and weather would alter the mound before modern excavation began.")
r(7, 'P7-HYMN-CALLBACK', 'the kind that woman was singing in the doorway.', 'a real surviving piece of music to set beside our imagined doorway singer.')
r(7, 'P7-PLEA-OUTCOME', "Ugarit's plea for help saved nothing. One of its songs got through.", "The kingdom ended. One of its songs remained in the clay.")
p(7, 'P7-DURATION', 'It took about fifty years.', "The changes unfolded across several decades, and not everywhere at the same pace. Different sites have different histories, and the dates are often less precise than a timeline makes them look.")
r(7, 'P7-AUDIT-SCOPE', 'did something nobody had done.', 'reexamined a large body of destruction claims.')
p(7, 'P7-EMAR-RIVER', 'The first is a town called Emar,', "The first is Emar, a town on the Euphrates in inland Syria. A river connected it to other places, but it was far from the Mediterranean coast. Its end in the early twelfth century cannot be explained simply by imagining ships beaching beneath its walls.")
p(7, 'P7-GRAIN-PRICE', 'In the year of distress, a man sold his house.', "Some of the contracts record people selling property or entering dependence during desperate conditions. Grain prices and the terms of particular transactions need to be read in their own documents. We should not turn a price from one tablet into an exact daily diet and monthly wage for every family in the town.")
p(7, 'P7-CHILDREN', "And there's one contract I find very hard", "One contract records a couple selling four children, two boys and two girls, including a daughter still nursing. It names the parents and the children. The family explains its desperate need, and the transaction is recorded with witnesses. The people involved have not disappeared into an anonymous number.")
p(7, 'P7-FOOTPRINTS', "A child can't sign a contract", "The document says the parents pressed their children's feet into clay. Those impressions helped identify the children in the transaction. We should not invent a scene in which each child signs a contract or the scribe presses every foot. But the little clay footprints are real.")
s(7, 'P7-HATTUSA', 'For a century people assumed it was stormed', 'One branch of that family did survive.', """The evidence does not fit a simple picture of a full palace taken by storm. Important buildings had been largely cleared of valuable contents before they burned. Different parts of the city did not necessarily empty or burn together.

Someone had removed things worth carrying. We can picture the practical work of leaving: animals loaded, doors opened, objects selected and others abandoned. We cannot give that departure a precise day, an inventory of everything the court took, or a known destination. The archaeology suggests withdrawal and disruption rather than supplying a witnessed last procession.

The central government disappeared from its old capital. The political and cultural world it had helped create did not vanish everywhere with it.

""")
p(7, 'P7-CARCHEMISH', 'One branch of that family did survive.', "At Carchemish, a city on the Euphrates ruled by a branch of the Hittite royal family, rulers continued to claim that inheritance. The name is worth keeping because it prevents us from confusing the end of the capital with the disappearance of every Hittite successor.")
r(7, 'P7-PYLOS-DATE', 'Around 1180 BCE the palace burned,', 'Around the beginning of the twelfth century BCE the palace burned,')
p(7, 'P7-PYLOS-CLAY', "Here's the part I told you to remember.", "Remember the unbaked tablets in the palace archive. The fire hardened many of them. Working records that had not been made as a history book survived because of the building's destruction. The same event that ended the administration preserved much of what we know about its work.")
p(7, 'P7-TIRYNS', 'The other Greek palaces went in the same decades.', "Other Greek palace centers suffered destruction and major change in these generations. But at Tiryns, the place with the massive walls, the town expanded after the palace burned. People built new houses and continued living there. That doesn't reveal every detail of their leadership or record-keeping. It does show why the death of a palace and the death of a community are different events.")
p(7, 'P7-EGYPT-TRANSITION', 'Now Egypt, because Egypt is where', "Now turn south to Egypt, where a king left his own very different account of these years.")
p(7, 'P7-MIGRATION', "That's a migration. Those are families", "The carts and children suggest movement involving families, not only fighting men. They do not tell us exactly where each family began, what occupation it had, or why it left. I think of our imagined Farmer and his seed jar. That is one possible human story to place beside the image, not an identification supplied by the carving.")
p(7, 'P7-SETTLEMENT', "Egypt didn't drive them all into the sea.", "Egyptian royal texts also claim that captives were settled in strongholds and supplied with rations. Exactly where particular groups were placed, and how those claims relate to the later Philistine towns on the southern Levantine coast, is debated. The name Peleset is important to that discussion, but the texts do not give us a simple settlement map.")
p(7, 'P7-EGYPT-TRADE', 'Egypt survived. It kept its river,', "Egypt survived, with kings, temples, writing, and a great agricultural base. It also lost much of its earlier power abroad and faced serious difficulties at home. The story is of a state under strain, not a country with no one left to trade with.")
p(7, 'P7-ELAM-DYNASTY', 'One more, far to the east,', "One more case, far to the east. In the middle of the twelfth century BCE, Elamite rulers from southwestern Iran attacked Babylonia. A succession of campaigns ended the Kassite dynasty that had ruled there for centuries. This was a different political history from an attack on a Mediterranean port.")
p(7, 'P7-LOOT', 'That king of Elam liked souvenirs.', "Elamite kings carried prestigious monuments back to Susa, their center in southwestern Iran. Among the objects taken east were the victory monument of Naram-Sin, the king with the horned helmet, and the great stone inscribed with Hammurabi's laws.")
p(7, 'P7-LOOT-LOCATION', "That's why Hammurabi's pillar was found in Iran.", "That is why these monuments of Mesopotamian kings were recovered at Susa in Iran. They had acquired a second life as another dynasty's trophies. Both are now in the Louvre; we do not need to put them in one ancient storeroom to understand what their journey means.")
s(7, 'P7-ENDING', 'So who did it? That\'s the wrong kind of question,', '[VISUAL: Part card — THE FAMILY, 1150 BCE.', """So who did it? A single culprit cannot explain these different endings. Some places were attacked. Some ruling institutions withdrew or failed. Environmental pressures mattered, but not in exactly the same way everywhere.

Connections could carry trouble: a force sent away, a shipment delayed, an institution unable to pay its workers. They could also bring help, alternative suppliers, and new opportunities. The evidence we have just followed is a patchwork of destruction, adaptation, and survival. Any explanation has to fit that patchwork.

""")
p(7, 'P7-KEEPER', 'The Keeper is gone.', "At Ugarit and Pylos the old royal administrations are gone. Elsewhere kings survive, including successors in Egypt and the rulers of Carchemish. The Keeper's kind of work has not disappeared, but the person doing it, and the territory he can command, may have changed.")
p(7, 'P7-COUNTER', 'The Counter has a skill nobody is hiring.', "At a destroyed Greek palace, the Counter has lost the institution that sustained his work. That can interrupt the training of another generation. In Egypt and Mesopotamia, scribal traditions continue. The fate of writing depends on which rooms, patrons, and communities survive.")
p(7, 'P7-CARRIER', 'The Carrier has ships, and no ports', "The Carrier may have lost a customer, a partner, or a familiar destination. Some routes become dangerous or unprofitable; others continue or change. I suspect some people moved between trade and raiding. We cannot give that biography to every captain whose name has vanished from an archive.")
p(7, 'P7-MAKER', 'The Maker has no tin.', "A smith whose supplies are interrupted can repair old objects and melt down scrap. Bronze working and exchange do not cease everywhere. A hoard tells us that metal was valuable enough to keep; it does not tell us the owner's exact plan or whether that person survived.")
p(7, 'P7-CHARIOTS', 'Chariots went with him, by the way.', "The loss of a palace can also damage the workshops, stables, and provisioning that sustain its chariot forces. Chariots themselves do not disappear from the ancient world. Their history continues under other rulers.")
p(7, 'P7-FARMER', 'The Farmer is alive.', "Many farming households survive; others are killed, displaced, or forced to change where and how they live. Losing a collector might mean keeping more grain. Losing a water supply, a market, or protection might mean the opposite. We need to follow particular places before deciding what the collapse did for ordinary people.")
p(7, 'P7-SISTER-CATEGORIES', "She's been in the story since the first house,", "The sister has been our way of keeping women's labor in view, from grinding grain to textile production. These are different women in different societies and legal positions. At Pylos, the ration records give us a glimpse of groups attached to palace work. They do not give us a continuous biography of the woman at the grinding stone.")
p(7, 'P7-WOMENS-EVIDENCE', "We don't know, and the reason matters.", "For most of the particular women represented in those lists, we cannot follow what happened next. The records tell us what an administration needed to record, not everything there was to know about a life. When that administration stops leaving records, our view becomes much poorer.")
p(7, 'P7-NAMED', 'For four thousand years they were counted and never named.', "Some ancient women and dependent people do survive by name. We have just met children named in an Emar contract. What we so rarely recover is their own account of the decisions made about them.")
s(7, 'P7-LAST-BRIDGE', "By 1150 BCE it's quiet.", 'Next time, the world afterward.', """By the middle of the twelfth century, the map has changed profoundly. At some ruined palaces the administrative record ends. In other towns people are building, trading, governing, and writing. Their lives do not all fit into the silence of an abandoned archive.

In Greece, memories of powerful kings and great buildings will enter stories passed from one generation to the next. Elsewhere, institutions carry older traditions forward more directly. The next question is what people can rebuild from the resources, skills, and relationships that remain.

""")

# Part 8: survival is visible, and a changed political order is not universal freedom.
p(8, 'P8-IMAGINED-HERDER', 'Somewhere around the year 1000 BCE', "Picture a herder below the walls of Tiryns, in southern Greece, around 1000 BCE. We are roughly two centuries after the destruction of its Bronze Age palace. The person is imagined; the massive walls are real.")
p(8, 'P8-WALL-CONDITION', "He's in the south of Greece,", "The wall rises above him in huge, irregular stones fitted together without mortar. Some are as large as a loaded cart. The palace no longer operates as it once did, but people have continued living at Tiryns. A ruined royal center does not mean that every house around it is empty.")
p(8, 'P8-CYCLOPES', 'He knows who built it.', "Later Greeks would explain walls like these as the work of Cyclopes, one-eyed giants. We cannot date that story to this particular herder's lifetime. But stand beneath the stones for a moment and you can feel the question that invites it: who could organize the work of getting something that heavy up there?")
p(8, 'P8-HERDER-KNOWLEDGE', "He's wrong, and you know he's wrong,", "The builders were people, using human labor and organization. What later observers could no longer see was the working arrangement behind the masonry: the food, tools, supervision, and repeated effort. A wall can outlast the community's knowledge of how it was made.")
p(8, 'P8-LITERACY-SIGNS', 'The grandfather is the clerk at the desk', "The grandfather is a palace clerk. His script has about ninety syllabic signs, alongside signs for commodities and numbers. The surviving texts are overwhelmingly accounts. He learned a specialized practice supported by the palace, though we cannot reconstruct every detail of how scribes were taught.")
p(8, 'P8-LITERACY-NO-NEED', 'The palace burns when his son', "In this imagined family, the palace burns while the son is still learning the work. He gets away, but has to make a living in a different way. He remembers signs he no longer uses regularly. No institution pays him to finish his training or teach somebody else.")
p(8, 'P8-LITERACY-SCOPE', "That's all it takes: three generations", "That is one way to picture a break in transmission. The evidence shows the disappearance of Linear B administration after the palaces, not the thoughts of this invented family. When Greek alphabetic writing becomes visible centuries later, it uses a different system adopted from elsewhere.")
p(8, 'P8-REBUILDING', "The Maker can't cast bronze without tin.", "Rebuilding royal administration means more than repairing a roof. People need workers, supplies, customers, protection, and an authority they are willing or able to support. Some of those connections survive. Others have to be made again. A household can rebuild its own room without being able to reconstruct a kingdom.")
p(8, 'P8-LEFKANDI-BURIAL', 'On the island of Euboea,', "On Euboea, an island close to the eastern coast of mainland Greece, excavators at Lefkandi uncovered a very long building dating to around 950 BCE. Timber posts and mud-brick walls once stood above its stone foundations. Inside were exceptional burials. This was substantial construction in a period often imagined as nothing but tiny poor villages.")
p(8, 'P8-LEFKANDI-CLAIMS', 'So in the darkest part of the dark age,', "Someone here had access to wealth, skilled work, and objects whose histories reached across the sea. We should not turn that into an exact workforce or a complete biography of a chief. The burial and building are enough to interrupt the idea that every connection had gone.")
p(8, 'P8-BASILEUS', 'The kings of the Greek dark age were the descendants', "The title changed its range of meaning. That is not proof that every later king descended from a palace foreman, or that the highest surviving official automatically inherited a crown. It gives us a trace of continuity within a changed political world.")
p(8, 'P8-WENAMUN-TIME', 'Now I want you to meet a real person', "Now go back about a century from that burial and travel southeast across the Mediterranean, to Egypt. A text set around 1075 BCE follows an official named Wenamun on a difficult journey. It will put a face, and a grievance, into this world of continuing trade.")
a(8, 'P8-WENAMUN-SEIZURE', "Wenamun's trip goes wrong at once.", "The account then has him seize silver from people associated with the coastal group called the Tjeker, treating it as compensation for his loss. That matters later. The ships that pursue him are not simply an unexplained second disaster; he has created a dispute of his own.")
p(8, 'P8-WENAMUN-NEGATIVE', 'Then he has his clerks bring out', "The prince has records brought out showing what earlier Egyptian rulers supplied in exchange for timber. His argument is sharp: if he and his predecessors had simply been Pharaoh's servants, Pharaoh would not have needed to send payment. He rejects Wenamun's assumption that Egyptian prestige alone entitles him to the wood. The old records show exchange, not an earlier age of free cedar.")
p(8, 'P8-WENAMUN-VERDICT', "It's the best single document we have", "What I like about this account is that commerce keeps working inside the quarrel. There are ships, records, payments, and timber being cut. Egypt's standing is not what Wenamun thinks it should be, but the sea has not emptied and the little port has not forgotten how to keep accounts.")
p(8, 'P8-BYBLOS-ASIDE', 'One footnote. Those five hundred rolls', "The surviving text breaks off after further trouble, before a completed homecoming. We can leave the missing ending missing. Its value is the world it describes, not a tidy proof that every old power has been replaced by a free merchant.")
p(8, 'P8-PHOENICIAN-KINGS', 'They came through because they were small', "Tyre, Sidon, and Byblos were coastal cities in what is now Lebanon. Their people are often grouped under the name Phoenicians. They had rulers, institutions, crafts, and trading networks; they were not towns that had somehow escaped all royal power. Over the following centuries their seafarers expanded their activities westward across the Mediterranean. Political change could create opportunities without making every merchant independent of a king.")
p(8, 'P8-CARRIER-FREEDOM', "So the Carrier doesn't work for a king", "The Carrier's work continues under new conditions, sometimes in partnership with rulers and sometimes in private ventures. The contrast is with the vanished palace network, not with government of every kind.")
p(8, 'P8-HIGHLAND-WATER', 'Before the collapse almost nobody lived up there.', "The highlands had a history of settlement before the collapse. Surveys nevertheless show a marked expansion of small communities during the early Iron Age. The terrain made access to fields and water uneven. Springs, captured rainwater, storage, and the labor of working slopes mattered to where a household could live.")
p(8, 'P8-TAX-FREEDOM', "Now, here's the case for calling this a release.", "Here is the possibility that makes the idea of a release attractive. In some small communities, households may have had more control over their labor and produce than people attached to a palace estate. But a simple house and ordinary pottery do not prove that nobody collected dues, claimed land, or exercised power. We cannot read a tax exemption from the absence of a palace.")
p(8, 'P8-DARK-VERDICT', 'So was it dark? From a palace,', "So was it dark? The answer changes with the place and the life we are trying to recover. Some people may have gained room to act while losing security or access to goods. Others faced displacement and violence. I find that uneven picture more convincing than either a universal disaster or a universal liberation.")
r(8, 'P8-POTTERY-INFERENCE', "Foreign-style pots made on the spot mean foreign potters who've moved in.", 'Foreign-style pots made locally can suggest people or skills moving, but imitation and exchange have to be considered too.')
r(8, 'P8-DNA-SAMPLE', "Two centuries later it's faded to nothing. They'd married into the neighborhood.", 'In the later people sampled, that distinct signal was no longer detectable in the same way, consistent with mixing into the local population.')
p(8, 'P8-IRON-SUPPLY', "You know his problem. There's no tin.", "A smith in a disrupted community may have difficulty obtaining familiar supplies. Some bronze production and trade continue, especially in places such as Cyprus. The question is why iron becomes more widely useful alongside them, not how every smith instantly replaces a metal that has disappeared.")
p(8, 'P8-IRON-CAUSE', 'Why then? The usual answer', "Disruption to supplies is one proposed part of the story, but it cannot explain the whole transition. Iron ores are more widely available than tin, while turning them into dependable tools demands fuel, furnaces, skill, and repeated work. Craftspeople in different places solve those problems at different times. A nearby ore deposit helps; it does not make a working iron industry appear by itself.")
p(8, 'P8-STEEL', "It wasn't better at first.", "Early iron was not automatically better than good bronze. Heating iron in contact with charcoal can add carbon; controlled heating and cooling can then produce and harden steel. Simply plunging any piece of hot iron into water does not guarantee a superior blade. This is knowledge built through practice, and its adoption is uneven rather than one invention arriving everywhere in a particular year.")
p(8, 'P8-IRON-ACCESS', 'Think what that means for the Farmer.', "Over time, wider iron production changes the tools a farming household can obtain. That matters enormously. It does not mean bronze had belonged only to kings, or that every village suddenly possessed an independent smith with all the ore and fuel he needed.")
p(8, 'P8-IRON-PROMISE', "I said I'd tell you why the Iron Age began.", "That is the answer I can defend: changing supply networks, accumulated technical skill, and the wider usefulness of a different metal. The transition took generations. The name Iron Age makes it sound like a switch being thrown; the objects show people working across the change.")
p(8, 'P8-ALPHABET-SCRIBES', 'For six hundred years after that,', "Early alphabetic writing survives in scattered inscriptions over the following centuries. Their small number makes its users hard to reconstruct. It was not simply ignored by every professional scribe: remember Ugarit's alphabet written in wedges on clay, already used before the collapse. Different systems could coexist because they served different languages, traditions, and institutions.")
p(8, 'P8-ALPHABET-CAUSE', 'Then the palaces fell, and the schools', "The political disruptions changed the institutions supporting different scripts. In several coastal communities, alphabetic writing continued and developed. A set of roughly twenty-two consonant signs became characteristic of Phoenician writing, carried through trade and other contact. Fewer basic signs can make learning the system easier; useful literacy still requires practice, and it was never automatically shared by every household.")
p(8, 'P8-ALPHABET-CREDIT', "It's the most important invention in this whole series,", "For me, the alphabet is one of this story's most consequential inventions. Its early history shows people adapting an existing technology to their own language. Its later success involves traders, rulers, scribes, and communities, not just one profession losing its monopoly in a fire.")
p(8, 'P8-WHATIF', "Here's a what-if that I can't prove", "Here is a what-if. Suppose more of the palaces had survived. Would alphabetic writing and iron have spread through the same places at the same speed? Perhaps the opportunities would have been different. But both developments had histories before the destruction, so we cannot make catastrophe their necessary inventor. The interesting question is how people used changing conditions, not whether suffering had to happen to give us letters and tools.")
p(8, 'P8-EAST', 'Not everybody forgot. In the east,', "Not everybody lost the same institutions. Assyria and Babylonia, in what is now Iraq, retained kingship and cuneiform learning through periods of serious disruption and change. Egypt continued its own written traditions. These are different histories from the disappearance of Greek palace administration.")
p(8, 'P8-MEMORY-MAP', 'That difference decides what comes next.', "Those differences matter for memory. Some knowledge travels through schools and archives; some through stories, ritual, and practical work. In the last part we will ask what each route preserves, and what it leaves out.")
s(8, 'P8-CLOSING-HOUSE', "So where's the family, around 750 BCE?", 'So what did survive? And how,', """By around 750 BCE, several centuries after the palace destructions, this is no longer simply the first generation making do among ruins. New kingdoms and communities have formed. Trade has expanded in some regions. Greek alphabetic writing is beginning to leave us inscriptions.

Picture a farming household in that changing world. There is still grain to store and grind. Tools have to be repaired, and somebody has to deal with a trader or a person claiming a share of the crop. A mark on a jar may tell us that someone used writing; it cannot tell us that everyone in the house could read or that nobody went to a teacher.

We can recognize our five kinds of work without forcing them all back under one roof. Some are local. Others still reach across long distances. The old palaces have not returned in the same form, but neither has the world become a collection of households that need nothing from anyone else.

That is the world in which stories about earlier kings and their great buildings will be told, retold, and eventually written down.

""")

# Part 9: which story survives, how decipherment works, and an ending earned by the evidence.
s(9, 'P9-TROY-OPENING', 'You know this story too.', "Here's where we are.", """You may know parts of this story. A queen called Helen leaves with a prince of Troy. Greek kings take an army across the sea. Achilles fights, a wooden horse helps bring the war to an end, and the city falls.

But that familiar version combines different ancient works and later retellings. The Iliad, traditionally attributed to Homer, tells a much shorter episode near the end of the war, centered on Achilles's anger. It does not narrate the wooden horse or make him invulnerable except at his heel. Keeping that distinction matters, because we are about to ask what a poem can remember.

""")
p(9, 'P9-HOMER-CAPTIVES', "There's no clerk in Homer,", "Homer's world includes captives, weaving, household labor, and kings. What it does not give us is the administrative world recovered from the palace tablets: the particular system of officials, allocations, and working records. We should not claim to know everything the poet knew. We can compare what the poems preserve with the quite different things the archives reveal.")
p(9, 'P9-HELMET-INFERENCE', 'So a description of an object passed', "The helmet is a striking connection between the poem and the material world of the palaces. Oral tradition may have carried the description; surviving old objects or later encounters with them are other possibilities. The resemblance is evidence of a connection, not a recording of twenty generations of singers passing along exactly the same words.")
p(9, 'P9-TROY-VERDICT', "So here's what I think. There was no", "Here is what I think the comparison gives us. A real place, political conflict in the surrounding region, and contacts with the Greek world make a historical background for the tradition plausible. They do not verify a ten-year siege, the wooden horse, or the biographies of Homer's heroes. We have found a world in which stories like this could take root, not the war's official paperwork.")
p(9, 'P9-HITTITE-PAPERWORK', 'I love the shape of this.', "I love the shape of that discovery. An archive from a different kingdom helps us investigate the geography and politics behind a Greek tradition. The names are clues to a relationship between history and story, not permission to identify every person in one with a character in the other.")
p(9, 'P9-NESTOR-GRAVE', "In a grave on that island, a boy's grave,", "In a grave on that island, archaeologists found a small clay drinking cup with lines of verse scratched on it. The burial was long described as a boy's, but reexamination of the cremated remains identified several individuals. The inscription on the cup remains one of the earliest examples of Greek alphabetic writing. Its joke matters more here than an unsupported biography of its owner.")
p(9, 'P9-WRITING-OWNERSHIP', "Think about where that cup stands", "Think about where that cup stands in our story. The palace tablets of Pylos belong to a specialized administrative practice. Elsewhere we have already met private letters, poetry, and contracts long before this cup. Writing had never remained only a receipt for barley. The cup shows a different use in early Greek alphabetic writing: verse attached to an object people could handle at a gathering.")
p(9, 'P9-LITERACY-WEEKS', 'Then it was lost in Greece.', "After the disappearance of Linear B, Greek alphabetic inscriptions begin to survive centuries later. A smaller set of signs does not make everyone literate in a few weeks. But these early inscriptions show people putting writing to uses far beyond a palace's accounts.")
p(9, 'P9-CUP-PAYOFF', 'The counting had turned into writing', "Someone wanted words on a cup to do something to its audience. They were making a joke, invoking a famous name, and playing with a promise of desire. The object gives us a little social moment as well as a place in the history of writing.")
p(9, 'P9-ASSYRIA-RETURN', 'Remember Assur, the merchant city', "Remember Assur, the merchant city on the Tigris where Lamassi lived. The Assyrian kingdom in that northern homeland survived the upheavals around 1200 BCE and later expanded into a great empire. By the seventh century its rulers had made Nineveh one of their most important centers. We have returned to the world whose ruins opened this series.")
p(9, 'P9-LIBRARY-CHAIN', 'That library is the end of an unbroken line.', "That collection depended on generations of copying, teaching, collecting, and selection. Mesopotamian scribal traditions survived the disruptions around 1200 BCE; that does not mean one school stayed open unchanged for thousands of years. People and institutions kept doing the work of transmission.")
p(9, 'P9-XENOPHON', 'Two hundred and eleven years later,', "About two centuries later, Xenophon and the Greek army passed ruins generally identified with Nineveh. His brief account recorded walls and stories, not an excavation of the city.")
p(9, 'P9-LIBRARY-LOCATION', 'He was standing on top of it.', "Somewhere within those ruins lay tablets from the royal collections, broken and buried, with texts that could illuminate a history his account barely recovered. We cannot put his feet directly above the library or say that he asked questions it would answer line by line. The contrast is strong enough without that invented precision: records had survived where a passing traveler could not read them.")
p(9, 'P9-BIBLE-FRAME', 'I want to go carefully here.', "The Hebrew Bible is sacred to a great many people. Here I am asking a historical question: how do its stories, laws, and literary forms relate to older traditions in the Near East? Parallels can show a shared cultural world without giving us a simple route of borrowing for every passage.")
r(9, 'P9-RELEASE-WORD', 'The Hebrew word for that release is the same word the Babylonians used.', 'The Hebrew term for release is related to an Akkadian term used for release; the institutions and texts are not identical.')
p(9, 'P9-SLAVES-VOICE', "And there's one thing in it that I don't think", "One feature of the Exodus story especially matters to me. It asks its audience to remember enslavement and to identify with people escaping oppression. That does not establish that the surviving text was written by the enslaved people themselves, or that no older work ever spoke from below. It is a powerful use of remembered vulnerability in a tradition carried forward by later generations.")
p(9, 'P9-HIEROGLYPHS', 'Take Egypt first. We know the day', "Take Egypt first. The last known dated hieroglyphic inscription was carved at Philae in southern Egypt in 394 CE. That dates a surviving inscription, not the day the last reader died. Knowledge of the script faded over time, while the Egyptian language continued in a later form called Coptic. That surviving language would help make decipherment possible.")
p(9, 'P9-NAMES-DECIPHERMENT', 'The key was names.', "Royal names were crucial clues. The Greek text named Ptolemy, and groups of Egyptian signs enclosed in ovals could be compared with that name and others, including Cleopatra on a different monument. Repeated signs helped establish sound values. Names opened a door, but Coptic and the grammar of the language helped scholars go through it.")
p(9, 'P9-1857-SCOPE', "That's the day I'd point to if someone asked", "That test gave strong public support to the decipherment of Akkadian cuneiform. Other scripts and languages needed their own breakthroughs and independent tests. Reading a clay tablet is not one solved puzzle applied unchanged to every civilization.")
p(9, 'P9-SMITH-ANECDOTE', 'A colleague who was in the room', "A later account describes Smith becoming so excited that he rushed around the room and began removing his clothes. It is a wonderful story, but we should introduce it as a later recollection rather than a contemporary transcript. What is secure is the discovery that made the excitement understandable: he had recognized a Mesopotamian flood story.")
p(9, 'P9-SMITH-XENOPHON', "He'd found the flood.", "He had found a version of the flood tradition among the tablets recovered from Nineveh. The ruined city in the opening of this series was yielding words again.")
r(9, 'P9-SCRIPT-LANGUAGE', 'so they could pronounce every word.', 'so they could identify many signs and approximate sounds without yet understanding the language.')
p(9, 'P9-GREEK-PROOF', 'So the people of the palaces were Greeks.', "The tablets recorded an early form of Greek. That changed the history of the language and the interpretation of the palace world. It did not prove that every hero in Homer had existed. The test worked because a proposed reading predicted ordinary words on another tablet: pots, handles, and a vessel with three legs.")
p(9, 'P9-CLAY', 'Clay is the cheapest thing anyone ever wrote on.', "Clay could be an inexpensive, readily available writing material. Under favorable conditions it preserves writing for extraordinary lengths of time. Fire can harden an unbaked tablet, though heat, collapse, water, and later damage can also destroy one. The surviving archives are a remarkable inheritance, not proof that clay makes every message permanent.")
p(9, 'P9-CONNECTION-LESSON', 'The first: every connection that made them richer', "The first: connections create capabilities as well as obligations. A household or workshop can obtain things it cannot produce alone. When a route or a partner fails, that can become a vulnerability. Other connections may offer a way through. The question is which relationships survive and what alternatives remain.")
p(9, 'P9-RESILIENCE-LESSON', 'The second: a strong system hides its own trouble.', "The second: recognizing danger is not the same as being able to solve it. The letters show people asking for grain, ships, payment, and help. Stores and alliances could absorb difficulties, but neither guaranteed that the next crisis would be manageable. We should not mistake their failure for proof that nobody noticed.")
p(9, 'P9-SURVIVAL-LESSON', "The third: when it broke, what saved people", "The third: survival took different forms. A household might rely more heavily on local resources; a port might endure because it kept trading; a state might preserve institutions that another lost. There is no single recipe of self-sufficiency hidden inside all those different outcomes.")
p(9, 'P9-MEMORY-LESSON', 'And the fourth: memory lasts only', "And the fourth: memory requires carriers. People teach, copy, tell stories, preserve objects, and give old words new uses. Different carriers keep different things. An account tablet, a poem, and a sacred text are not interchangeable windows into the same past.")
p(9, 'P9-MODERN-CONTRAST', "In another way it's completely different,", "We also possess something Xenophon did not: ways to bring excavated objects, deciphered texts, and environmental evidence into the same investigation. Earlier people preserved real knowledge as well as stories. Our advantage is not that everyone before us guessed wrong, but that we can compare more kinds of evidence and test more of the guesses.")
p(9, 'P9-INDUS-SEAL-DATE', 'Far to the east of everything in this story,', "Far to the east, the Indus civilization built cities with planned streets and elaborate drainage long before Hammurabi's reign. Its people traded with Mesopotamia. Remember the interpreter of Meluhha, known from a seal around the end of the third millennium BCE. The Indus signs survive on seals and other objects, but there is still no generally accepted decipherment.")
p(9, 'P9-LAST-LIBRARY', "Somewhere there's a mound nobody has dug,", "Perhaps another discovery will change that. Perhaps the missing clue was written on something that did not survive. We don't know. The signs are still there, and so is the question of what their makers meant. That is a good place to leave a story about recovering the past: with something real in our hands that we still cannot read.")

